{
  "requirement": "A provider failure that ends the model conversation returns\n`ValidationError::Agent` to the awaiting ask builder.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The requirement has one obligation: a provider failure that ends the model conversation returns `ValidationError::Agent` to the awaiting ask builder.\n\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/tests.rs#fn[assistant_text_and_provider_failure_cannot_become_typed_results]` covers the main case. It builds a `ToolAgent` over `Script::new(vec![None])`. The `Script` provider's `complete` returns `io::Error::other(\"permanent provider failure\")` when the next turn is `None`. The test awaits `ctx.ask::<bool>()` and asserts the result matches `Err(ValidationError::Agent(error))` with the provider's message in the error text. It also asserts that no dependencies were recorded. This exercises the failure path and checks both the error variant and that the provider's error is passed through.\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/configured.rs#mod[tests]/fn[configured_agents_initialize_only_when_asked_and_report_setup_errors]` adds a second scenario. A configured agent with a missing credential makes the awaited `validator.verify` call fail. The test asserts `ValidationError::Agent(_)` and that the message names the missing credential variable. This is a provider setup failure, and it is surfaced through the awaited ask in the same way.\n\nThe text does not distinguish failures by when they occur, such as mid-conversation or after tool calls. It also does not distinguish transient from permanent failures. Both tests marked `{test}` fail the call as the requirement states, so I found no unexercised obligation or failure case.",
